{"step_index":234,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-21T19:38:07Z","thinking":"**Assessing Bottlenecks**\n\nOkay, I've got the initial OpenRouter script run analyzed; it functions, but it's painfully slow, taking ages even for a small dataset. The issue is clearly a performance bottleneck, and now I'm thinking about where that slowdown is occurring, and how to improve it.\n\n\n**Investigating Optimization Options**\n\nOkay, so I've been thinking about the slowness and have some ideas. The current approach, throwing everything at the LLM for cross-attention, is clearly the bottleneck. It's an O(N x M) operation, which is insane. I'm exploring the vector database, or embeddings-based search, since that would be a huge improvement. I'll be creating embeddings for the warehouse items and then using those embeddings for searching to make the LLM process smaller tasks.\n\n\n**Designing the Hybrid Approach**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a hybrid strategy. My thought is, that I can use the vector similarity search, perhaps with a simple cosine similarity matrix with NumPy, as a fast initial filter. This involves embedding the shortage items and warehouse items, then finding the top-K closest matches for each shortage. A further refinement would involve only passing those top-K items to the LLM for the final match decision, drastically reducing the LLM's workload. I have thought about trying a faster pure Python alternative, such as TF-IDF combined with N-gram matching.\n\n\n**Refining Search Strategies**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on optimizing the retrieval process. I was stuck thinking the dataset was huge, but it's only 207 items. So, the 30-minute delay wasn't the data size, it's OpenRouter's congestion and slow model speeds. The free models are slow, with each request taking 1-2 minutes. To solve this, I'm thinking of concurrent API calls with `ThreadPoolExecutor` to drastically reduce processing time. I also see that semantic search using TF-IDF or vector embeddings locally is very promising for quickly finding the top candidates.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Matching Methods**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into optimization. I've realized I have `rapidfuzz`! Why not use it to pre-filter, sending only the top 3 candidates to the LLM? My current approach involves passing the *entire* warehouse catalog to the LLM for every batch, which is insane. I am thinking that `Shortage_A` -> `Fuzzy Top 5 in Warehouse` -> `LLM (Yes/No)` is a good option. I am also aware that Arabic drug names might be missed if spelled completely differently.\n\n\n**Validating Optimizations**\n\nI am now focusing on threading to fix immediate performance issues. Wrapping batch processing in a `ThreadPoolExecutor` seems promising, potentially slashing processing time. I need to test if the rate limits of the `openrouter/free` tier will allow this concurrency. After this, I'll validate the user's vector database idea, converting warehouse items into numerical vectors.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Vector Search**\n\nI'm now implementing the user's vector database suggestion. My plan involves a classic Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) approach to address the N x M complexity. Specifically, I plan to convert both shortages and warehouse items into vectors, followed by using the top-3 closest warehouse vectors for each shortage. These top matches will then be passed to the LLM for a final, quick decision.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"replace_file_content","args":{"AllowMultiple":false,"Description":"Implement multithreading to speed up LLM batch matching","EndLine":265,"Instruction":"Use concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor to run batches in parallel.","ReplacementContent":"    def _llm_batch_match(\n        self,\n        shortage_items: list[dict],\n        warehouse_items: list[dict],\n        warehouse_name: str,\n    ) -> list[dict]:\n        import concurrent.futures\n\n        batch_size = 50\n        all_results = []\n\n        wid = \"W1\"\n        catalog = [\n            {\"id\": f\"{wid}-{i:05}\", \"name\": item.get(\"item_name_raw\", \"\")}\n            for i, item in enumerate(warehouse_items, 1)\n        ]\n        catalog_by_id = {item[\"id\"]: item[\"name\"] for item in catalog}\n\n        cases = []\n        for i, item in enumerate(shortage_items, 1):\n            cases.append({\n                \"case_id\": f\"Q{i:05}-{wid}\",\n                \"requested_name\": item.get(\"item_name_raw\", \"\"),\n                \"catalog_id\": wid,\n            })\n\n        total_batches = (len(cases) + batch_size - 1) // batch_size\n        batches = []\n        for batch_idx in range(total_batches):\n            start = batch_idx * batch_size\n            end = min(start + batch_size, len(cases))\n            batches.append((batch_idx, cases[start:end]))\n\n        print(f\"  -> [OpenRouter] Executing {total_batches} batches in parallel... This will be MUCH faster!\")\n\n        def process_batch(batch_data):\n            batch_idx, batch_cases = batch_data\n            inputs = {\"catalogs\": {wid: catalog}, \"cases\": batch_cases}\n            max_retries = 3\n            \n            for attempt in range(max_retries):\n                try:\n                    results = self._call_openrouter(inputs)\n                    parsed_results = []\n                    for case, decision in zip(batch_cases, results):\n                        shortage_name = case[\"requested_name\"]\n                        status = decision.get(\"status\", \"no_match\")\n                        item_ids = decision.get(\"item_ids\", [])\n                        warehouse_item = \"\"\n                        if isinstance(item_ids, list) and item_ids:\n                            warehouse_item = catalog_by_id.get(item_ids[0], \"\")\n                        parsed_results.append({\n                            \"shortage_name\": shortage_name,\n                            \"status\": status,\n                            \"warehouse_item\": warehouse_item,\n                        })\n                    print(f\"      [✓] Batch {batch_idx+1}/{total_batches} completed.\")\n                    return parsed_results\n                except Exception as e:\n                    if attempt < max_retries - 1:\n                        time.sleep(3.0)\n                    else:\n                        print(f\"      [!] Batch {batch_idx+1} failed: {e}\")\n            \n            # Fallback if completely failed\n            return [{\n                \"shortage_name\": case[\"requested_name\"],\n                \"status\": \"no_match\",\n                \"warehouse_item\": \"\"\n            } for case in batch_cases]\n\n        # Use ThreadPoolExecutor to run multiple requests at the same time\n        with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=6) as executor:\n            # Map returns results in the same order\n            results_list = list(executor.map(process_batch, batches))\n\n        for res in results_list:\n            all_results.extend(res)\n\n        return all_results","StartLine":159,"TargetContent":"    def _llm_batch_match(\n        self,\n        shortage_items: list[dict],\n        warehouse_items: list[dict],\n        warehouse_name: str,\n    ) -> list[dict]:\n        # Smaller batch size for free/open-source models so they don't lose context\n        batch_size = 50\n        all_results = []\n\n        wid = \"W1\"\n        catalog = [\n            {\"id\": f\"{wid}-{i:05}\", \"name\": item.get(\"item_name_raw\", \"\")}\n            for i, item in enumerate(warehouse_items, 1)\n        ]\n        catalog_by_id = {item[\"id\"]: item[\"name\"] for item in catalog}\n\n        cases = []\n        for i, item in enumerate(shortage_items, 1):\n            cases.append({\n                \"case_id\": f\"Q{i:05}-{wid}\",\n                \"requested_name\": item.get(\"item_name_raw\", \"\"),\n                \"catalog_id\": wid,\n            })\n\n        total_batches = (len(cases) + batch_size - 1) // batch_size\n        for batch_idx in range(total_batches):\n            start = batch_idx * batch_size\n            end = min(start + batch_size, len(cases))\n            batch_cases = cases[start:end]\n\n            print(f\"  -> [OpenRouter] Batch {batch_idx+1}/{total_batches} ({len(batch_cases)} items)... \", end=\"\", flush=True)\n\n            inputs = {\"catalogs\": {wid: catalog}, \"cases\": batch_cases}\n            \n            max_retries = 3\n            success = False\n            \n            for attempt in range(max_retries):\n                try:\n                    results = self._call_openrouter(inputs)\n                    for case, decision in zip(batch_cases, results):\n                        shortage_name = case[\"requested_name\"]\n                        status = decision.get(\"status\", \"no_match\")\n                        item_ids = decision.get(\"item_ids\", [])\n\n                        warehouse_item = \"\"\n                        if isinstance(item_ids, list) and item_ids:\n                            warehouse_item = catalog_by_id.get(item_ids[0], \"\")\n\n                        all_results.append({\n                            \"shortage_name\": shortage_name,\n                            \"status\": status,\n                            \"warehouse_item\": warehouse_item,\n                        })\n                    print(f\"OK\", flush=True)\n                    success = True\n                    break\n                \n                except Exception as e:\n                    error_msg = str(e)\n                    wait_time = 5.0\n                    if attempt < max_retries - 1:\n                        print(f\"\\n      [!] Error. Retrying in {wait_time:.1f}s... \", end=\"\", flush=True)\n                        time.sleep(wait_time)\n                    else:\n                        print(f\"\\n      [!] Failed: {error_msg}\", flush=True)\n                        break\n                        \n            if not success:\n                for case in batch_cases:\n                    if not any(r[\"shortage_name\"] == case[\"requested_name\"] for r in all_results):\n                        all_results.append({\n                            \"shortage_name\": case[\"requested_name\"],\n                            \"status\": \"no_match\",\n                            \"warehouse_item\": \"\",\n                        })\n\n        return all_results","TargetFile":"d:\\AI_Engineer\\Pharmacy-agy\\src\\match\\coordinator.py","toolAction":"Implement ThreadPoolExecutor","toolSummary":"Multithreading batches"}}]}
